  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Subject, object and possession",
      "summary": "Word order shows who does what, and the possessive shows who owns.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "In English the order of the words shows who does what, and the possessive shows who owns something."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**The dog** chased **the cat**.",
                "**The cat** chased **the dog**.",
                "the **dog's** bone"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "The subject comes before the verb and the object after it. Possession is shown with 's for people and animals, and with of for things: the leg of the table."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>subject, verb, object</td><td><strong>Anna loves Tom.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>'s possessive</td><td><strong>Anna's book</strong></td></tr><tr><td>of possessive</td><td><strong>the end of the day</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
